WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:03.780
Rhiannon: Children and young people
from the poorest households can be

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:07.090
up to three times more likely to
have a mental health problem than

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.959
those growing up in better-off homes.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.569
This may be related to the financial
strains and stresses put on the family.

00:00:17.529 --> 00:00:21.650
Parental mental illness is associated
with increased rates of mental health

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:26.310
problems in children and young people,
with an estimated one-third to two-thirds

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.589
of children and young people whose
parents have a mental health problem,

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:31.890
experiencing difficulty themselves.

00:00:35.369 --> 00:00:39.209
If the family is given support
financially and helped to overcome

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.490
these issues, then the potential
impact on mental health of the children

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.270
or young people should be lessened.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.400
A child cannot change their life in the
short term, but by getting the right

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.930
support from family and friends or even
from professionals, a child can be helped

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.220
to realise that they are not to blame,
and they can overcome these barriers.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:03.449
Physical well-being is a great way
towards improving mental wellbeing and if

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.190
children and young people are encouraged
to get involved with physical exercise,

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.749
this can help, as it influences the
release and uptake of the feel-good

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.020
chemicals ‘endorphins’ in the brain.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.840
By preventing or handling mental
health problems and building

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.440
resilience in children and young
people, their future mental health

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:24.160
and well-being can be improved.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.960
A child or young person’s ability
to manage emotions can support or

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.959
impede their learning, their academic
engagement, work ethic, commitment

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.440
and ultimately their school success.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:40.100
Schools need to educate children
and young people to understand how

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.320
to look after their mental health
as well as their physical health.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:49.110
Children with higher levels of emotional,
behavioural, social and school wellbeing,

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:53.802
have higher levels of academic achievement
and are more engaged in school.

00:01:53.802 --> 00:01:58.034
Emotional wellbeing is a key indicator
of academic achievement, and subsequently

00:01:58.034 --> 00:01:59.958
improved outcomes in later life.

00:01:59.958 --> 00:02:04.960
Good mental well-being on leaving
school has a much greater impact on life

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.440
chances and outcomes than exam success.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.700
Mental health and wellbeing programmes
in schools, can lead to significant

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.980
improvements in children’s mental
health, and social and emotional skills.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.749
Well-being provision can also
lead to reductions in classroom

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:21.889
misbehaviour and bullying.

00:02:22.949 --> 00:02:26.500
Schools are much more than centres
of learning and children need

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.600
an education that prepares them
academically and emotionally for the

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:33.720
challenges they will face inside the
classroom, and for when they leave.

